<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.6.3: Factoring the Difference of Squares</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01674</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.6.3: Factoring the Difference of Squares</md:title>
    <md:uuid>394b3fd6-9a28-42ea-ad0b-03fccb248457</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity introduces students to factoring a special product where a perfect square is subtracted from another
  perfect square, \(a^2 − b^2\). The pattern that represents the factoring of this binomial is \(a^2 − b^2 =
  (a − b)(a + b)\), where \((a − b)\) and \((a + b)\) are a conjugate pair.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Review the concept of conjugate pairs and introduce the difference of squares pattern as it is used to factor this
  special case of a binomial.</para>
<para>Place students into pairs to work on the questions 1 – 7 in the activity. Allow students to work together to
  factor the difference of squares.</para>
<para>Bring up question 7 to discuss as a class. Emphasize that the sum of two perfect squares does not match the pattern
  given for the difference of squares and therefore cannot be factored.</para>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for English Language Learners<title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para><title/><!--Extra Support Name-->MLR 3: Clarify, Critique, Correct: Conversing</para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body"> 
    <!--Support Content-->
    <para>After discussing question 7 as a class, present an incorrect answer and explanation. For example, "\(49x^2+16y^2 = (7x+4y)(7x+4y)\) because when you find the square in a polynomial expression you take the square of each term." Ask students to identify the error, critique the reasoning, and write a correct explanation. Invite students to share their critiques and corrected explanations with the class. Listen for and amplify the language students use to explain how to find the difference of squares. This helps students evaluate, and improve upon, the written mathematical arguments of others. </para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize"><!--Support Italics-->Design Principle(s): Optimize output (for explanation); Maximize meta-awareness</para>
    <para class="os-raise-extrasupport-title"><!--Extra Support Title-->Learn more about this routine</para>
    <!-- Extra Support Content-->
    <para><link url="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lozZJ21i3mQ;&amp;rel=0">View the instructional video</link> and <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1de908dfe7cb03fb7faad7f0277b41cf006d67a9">follow along with the materials</link> to assist you with learning this routine. </para>
    <para class="os-raise-extrasupport-title"><!--Extra Support Title-->Provide support for students</para>
    <!-- Extra Support Content-->
    <para><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f0d3544af9d0ff187158a6299ec77dc9d47e677e">Distribute graphic organizers</link> to the students to assist them with participating in this routine. </para>
  </div>
</div>

<title>Activity</title>
<para>Remember that \((a − b)\) and \((a + b)\) are a conjugate pair. When you multiply them together, you end up
  with the difference of squares, \(a^2 − b^2\).</para>
<para>Let’s look at the pattern used to factor a difference of squares:</para>
<para>\(a^2 − b^2 = (a − b)(a + b)\)</para>
<para>Work with a partner to factor using the difference of squares. Use the "^" symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
<para>As you factor each binomial, take turns with your partner, alternating the completion of each step one at a time. If
  you do not know the next step, ask your partner for help.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(81y^2 − 1\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((9y − 1)(9y + 1)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(196m^2 − 25n^2\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((14m − 5n)(14m + 5n)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(6p^2q^2 − 54p^2\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(6p^2(q − 3)(q + 3)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(7a^4c^2 − 7b^4c^2\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(7c^2(a − b)(a + b)(a^2 + b^2)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(a^2 + 6a + 9 − 9b^2\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((a + 3 − 3b)(a + 3 + 3b)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(x^2 + 6x + 9 − 4y^2\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((x + 3 − 2y)(x + 3 + 2y)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item> Can a binomial with an addition operation be factored using the difference of squares? Explain. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> No, the binomial that can be factored using a difference of squares will always have the
  form \((a^2 − b^2)\).</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Review the following key points from the lesson:</para>
<list>
  <item> The difference of squares pattern is \(a^2 − b^2 = (a − b)(a + b)\), where the difference of squares
    is factored into a conjugate pair. </item>
  <item> After factoring a difference of squares into binomials, multiply the binomials together to check your answer. </item>
  <item> The addition of two perfect squares in a binomial of the form \(a^2 + b^2\) is prime. </item>
</list>
<title>Video: Factoring the Difference of Squares</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about how to factor the difference of squares when there is also a greatest
  common factor.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <div class="os-raise-video-container">
    <video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
      <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3dd4ea7de318dc0911be9212995411f6c406a778">
